The capacitance is
\[
C=\epsilon_0\frac{A}{d}
=\frac{(8.85\times10^{-12})(0.040)}{2.0\times10^{-3}}
=1.77\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{F}
\]
The charge is
\[
Q=C\Delta V=(1.77\times10^{-10})(150)
=2.7\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{C}
\]
For a uniform field,
\[
E=\frac{\Delta V}{d}
=\frac{150}{2.0\times10^{-3}}
=7.5\times10^4\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}
\]

Rearrange the parallel-plate formula:
\[
C=\epsilon_0\frac{A}{d}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
A=\frac{Cd}{\epsilon_0}
\]
\[
A=\frac{(100\times10^{-12})(0.80\times10^{-3})}{8.85\times10^{-12}}
=9.0\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^2}
\]

For vacuum parallel plates,
\[
C=\epsilon_0\frac{A}{d}
\]
For capacitor B,
\[
A_B=2A_A,\qquad d_B=\frac{d_A}{2}
\]
Thus
\[
\frac{C_B}{C_A}
=\frac{A_B/d_B}{A_A/d_A}
=\frac{2A_A/(d_A/2)}{A_A/d_A}
=4
\]

First find the plate area:
\[
C=\epsilon_0\frac{A}{d}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
A=\frac{Cd}{\epsilon_0}
\]
\[
A=\frac{(220\times10^{-12})(1.2\times10^{-3})}{8.85\times10^{-12}}
=2.98\times10^{-2}\,\mathrm{m^2}
\]
The voltage is
\[
\Delta V=\frac{Q}{C}
=\frac{3.3\times10^{-9}}{220\times10^{-12}}
=15\,\mathrm{V}
\]
Thus
\[
E=\frac{\Delta V}{d}
=\frac{15}{1.2\times10^{-3}}
=1.25\times10^4\,\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}
\]

For large close plates, ignore fringing so the field between the plates is approximately uniform. The charge density is
\[
\sigma=\frac{Q}{A}
\]
The field between oppositely charged conducting plates is
\[
E=\frac{\sigma}{\epsilon_0}
=\frac{Q}{\epsilon_0A}
\]
The potential difference across a uniform field is
\[
\Delta V=Ed
=\frac{Qd}{\epsilon_0A}
\]
Therefore
\[
C=\frac{Q}{\Delta V}
=\frac{Q}{Qd/(\epsilon_0A)}
=\epsilon_0\frac{A}{d}
\]
